Modern QEMU test integration, not a production #27/#28 submission
================================================================

Branch test/riscv-final-stack-v5 carries separate modern manifest boot-tree
and SBI software-IPI adapters above #23-25 and #26. Neither adapter implements
AIA, general device assignment or ACPI synthesis. The parent endpoint is
pinned locally; tests keep the top-level host running. Shared receive-ring
reset does not cancel remote queued work or supply restart epochs (#7).

Private voluntary shutdown qualification
---------------------------------------

The adapter only permits a successful HALTED notice after normal shutdown
reaches its spawn-only syscore callback. The normal kernel halt/poweroff/reboot
paths have already called cpu_hotplug_disable() there. The callback rejects
panic and kexec, then trylocks the existing resource mutex and retains it until
terminal stop. This excludes local controllers and incoming resource mutations;
the parent must still confirm every assigned/tracked hart STOPPED. The resource
mutex is not acquired with a blocking wait in this callback or in machine-stop.

Direct machine-stop or emergency restart without prior qualification cannot
publish this notice. Neither can observed panic (even after qualification),
initialization failure or resource-lock contention.
SYSTEM_RESTART alone is not proof: emergency_restart() sets it without the
normal drain. Kexec also reaches syscore but later reopens CPU hotplug. Failure
to qualify or send must not prevent local/secondary HART_STOP. The parent keeps
ACTIVE ownership and allocations when settlement is unknown; direct execution
cannot bypass this retention. Automatic panic/lost-ACK recovery is unfinished.

panic_doorbell_test.sh executes the real registration, qualification and producer
code with controlled kernel boundaries. shutdown_test.sh checks local-stop
routing even when the notice helper returns; its counter measures attempts,
not successful publication. Neither test proves native hotplug locking, later
syscore callback ordering, live lockdep or firmware stop completion. Later
syscore callbacks run while the resource mutex is retained, so a newly enabled
callback with a conflicting dependency requires review and runtime qualification.
Old panic/lost-ACK recipes expecting reusable settlement do not qualify this
policy. The explicit retention recipes below replace those expectations;
fresh runtime evidence is still required.

Rejected-execution observations (private, default-off)
----------------------------------------------------

With mk_test_resource_trace=1, host instance 0 records a resource snapshot for
target instance 1 before returning EBUSY from ACTIVE execution. In this private
RISC-V build only, it then issues one raw HART_STATUS query for each physical
hart in the assigned/tracked union, including orphan tracking without duplicate
queries. The existing resource mutex remains held; no tracking, ownership,
allocation or lifecycle state is modified. There is no firmware polling loop.

Each MK_TEST_HSM line carries id, phase=exec-rejected, a monotonic sample number,
physical hart, state and raw SBI error. Nonzero error forces state=-1, never a
stopped claim from an undefined firmware return value. Sample numbers are
boot-local; the sequential statuses are not an atomic all-hart observation.
State and confirmation
snapshots outside exec-rejected do not add these queries. The per-object private
build flag and runtime switch must not appear in the production series.

These observations do not authorize reuse, cancel an outstanding operation or
prove no future start. Retention recipes must bound their retries, require a
complete fresh post-termination sample, correlate the frozen host/run identity,
and treat errors or uncertainty as missing stop proof while retaining resources.
The retention recipes below check these boundaries; neither old HSM traces nor
a unit fixture PASS establishes native stopped/reclaimed memory safety.

The qemu-host-init.py/qemu-child-init recipes extend Nickolaev's #35 harness
at 48507f176520b11a655344510b1b865b037e527f, with Kerf
38daa5687d6748846ffd003599a3bfba29785cd6. Use that harness's unchanged
scripts/build-initramfs.sh for fresh host/secondary output directories;
host payload is named /payload/vmlinux but contains the raw RISC-V Image.
Use the merged-/usr RISC-V runtime with Python, libfdt, Kerf and static BusyBox.

Run bash qemu_lifecycle_test.sh KERNEL_BUILD HARNESS KERF_SOURCE RISCV_RUNTIME
FIRMWARE OUTPUT_PARENT MODE HARTS CYCLES [ALTERNATE_BUILD] [FAULT_MODE]. OUTPUT_PARENT must end in /build;
every invocation creates a fresh bundle and preserves failures. It verifies
dependency pins, source cleanliness and exact Kerf runtime contents, reuses
the unchanged pinned packing helper, freezes inputs and verifies raw logs.
Build vmlinux/Image from the clean source immediately before packaging. The
optional alternate build must use the same clean source and config, but a
different LOCALVERSION (for example LOCALVERSION=-mk-alt) and distinct Image.
Alternate runs require at least two cycles. Each executing child's release
must match its selected frozen build; hashes must alternate at a fixed physical
Image address. Use CYCLES=50 for same and alternating replacement qualification.

Build CONFIG_MULTIKERNEL, CONFIG_MKTTY and CONFIG_KPROBES as built-ins. Boot
QEMU virt/PLIC with stock OpenSBI, four harts, 2GiB, no network/host disks,
console=ttyS0 rdinit=/init panic=0 kernelcore=1G log_buf_len=4M and
mk_lifecycle_guard=modern-v5. Options: mk_lifecycle_mode=graceful|halt|poweroff|
reboot, mk_lifecycle_harts=1|2 and mk_lifecycle_cycles=1..50. Panic uses the separate
two-hart, one-cycle panic-retain lane below, not reusable settlement.

The host must observe actual child PID1, exact assigned physical CPUs despite
different logical numbering, a ping/pong through mktty, fresh child boot IDs,
post-action HSM STOPPED evidence for every assigned hart and unchanged host
boot ID. On failure, retain the entire archive/config/firmware/command/log and
exit status. Host printk is retained in dmesg, not interleaved with structured
serial output; it is emitted after the final marker and checked for warnings.
A process exit zero alone is never a pass. These recipes do not
yet cover firmware fault injection, live CPU add,
native callback/RCU concurrency or physical cache behavior.

Test-only firmware selectors (not real SBI domain policy)
--------------------------------------------------------

Use a fresh OpenSBI worktree at 74434f255873d74e56cc50aa762d1caf24c099f8,
apply opensbi_test_hsm.patch with git apply --unidiff-zero and copy
opensbi_test_hsm.h to lib/sbi/. The context-free asset avoids treating unified
diff context indentation as source whitespace; the exact source pin is checked.
Only those
two paths may differ. Never reuse the historical dirty firmware checkout.
Run opensbi_fault_build.sh SOURCE KERNEL_BUILD MODE OUTPUT_PARENT to create
a fresh firmware bundle. Its priming selector is derived from the frozen ELF
and Image header for QEMU virt, not a historical literal address. It records
source, selector, patch, host hashes, command, build log and firmware hashes.

Modes: 0 off; 1 denied priming; 2 invalid priming; 3 second priming denied;
4 denied actual primary start; 5 persistently denied child-stub secondary starts
after primary start (host return to a different entry remains allowed);
6 synthetic STOP_PENDING for 32 polls after actual STOPPED, then resolves;
7 synthetic permanent STOP_PENDING after actual STOPPED. All selectors are
test-only. Modes 6/7 intentionally hide firmware's actual STOPPED state to
exercise host confirmation/retention, not emulate a hart still executing.
Unknown/sparse hart IDs are translated by firmware's mapping, never array
indexed directly. opensbi_fault_test.sh checks selectors at modeled boundaries.
Modes 1..4 are one-shot. Mode 5 defers its marker until a host status query,
avoiding firmware/host UART character interleaving during child boot logs.

Mode 6 uses the normal graceful/two-hart/one-cycle recipe and must additionally
pass verify_pending.py SERIAL_LOG EXIT_STATUS. It requires an injection marker
and a pending-to-stopped trace for each assigned hart; normal success without
fault injection is not accepted. Native denial/partial/stuck-state recipes use
MODE=deny-primary|partial-start|stuck-stop, HARTS=2, CYCLES=1, an empty alternate
build argument and FAULT_MODE=1..4|5|7 respectively. The frozen fault verifier
requires firmware injection markers, exact errno and confirmed cleanup for
denied starts; reduced online membership and every hart stopped after partial
start; and blocked exec/unload/kernel deletion with retained ownership for a
permanently pending report. Failed halt confirmation retains ACTIVE on this
source; do not fabricate FAILED to make a test pass. This exercises active
retention, not FAILED-state reclamation, which needs a separate reachable
test setup. Positive trace controls precede no-copy/no-start assertions.
Deletion writes an actual removal overlay through
current Kerf, not a userspace status veto or an unsupported instance rmdir.
No public bulk-unload syscall exists on this source; its generic free guard
requires separate test-only native instrumentation, not invented ID-zero ABI.
All executing cases also reject an active Image unload before termination.
Lost-ACK uses the explicit retention lane below. Do not count selector unit
checks or a firmware build as a native fault test.

Explicit retention modes (private test branch)
--------------------------------------------

Use HARTS=2, CYCLES=1, no alternate Image and one of:

  MODE=lost-ack     FAULT_MODE=0  matching shutdown ACK dropped, observed STOPPED
  MODE=lost-ack     FAULT_MODE=7  ACK dropped, synthetic persistent STOP_PENDING
  MODE=panic-retain FAULT_MODE=0  child SysRq panic with panic=1, observed STOPPED
  MODE=contended-retain FAULT_MODE=0  voluntary poweroff with actual mutex contention
  MODE=failed-retain FAULT_MODE=8 denied start, existing FAILED retention checks

The launcher rejects the old MODE=panic rather than relabeling its historical
reuse result. The child still receives the existing panic command. The verifier
requires one ordered child action, SysRq panic and reboot-timeout banner and
rejects panic diagnostics outside that interval. Lost-ACK requires the actual
matching successful ACK drop and 30-second timeout, not merely an error return.

ACTIVE lanes reject exec, unload and removal while retaining allocation and
ownership identities, including after the console reference is closed. A healthy
load/unload/delete control first proves release markers are live. Positive
copy/start traces precede termination. A separate continuous guarded trace must
remain free of copies/starts through termination and all rejected operations;
both raw trace blocks must match the recorded evidence and action intervals.
The guarded trace is read only after its END marker, not between operations
(reading trace pauses recording). Both ftrace boundary markers must survive,
entries-in-buffer must equal entries-written, and all four CPUs must report
zero overruns, commit overruns and dropped events. Tracing and both probes must
still be enabled at the endpoint. This accounts for the bounded observation
window, not indefinite future activity after it.

The pre-action ACTIVE snapshot supplies allocation identity only: ACTIVE is
published before architecture start fills hart tracking, so that snapshot may
have tracked=0. Fresh snapshots inside the anchored post-shutdown window must
all have tracked=2 and unchanged allocations. Contradictory states and cleared
resources inside the guard window are failures, never skipped discovery rows.

After the timeout/panic banner, host /dev/kmsg BEGIN/END records bind observations
to instance 1, mode and unchanged host boot ID. Rejected exec produces fresh
complete assigned/tracked HSM groups; userspace retries for at most a 20-second
deadline between calls, with a 100ms delay. This cannot bound a hung firmware
call; the outer QEMU timeout remains fail-closed. Missing, duplicate, stale,
wrong-hart, wrong-instance, incomplete or errored observations fail verification.
The last complete group must report STOPPED, or explicitly STOP_PENDING in the
fault-7 lane. STOP_PENDING is retention evidence, not host-confirmed stop proof.

Even an all-STOPPED observation does not authorize ACTIVE reuse. No RECOVERED
phase is accepted, resources remain until VM shutdown, and automatic panic or
lost-ACK recovery remains unfinished. FAILED handling and the dedicated
stuck-stop/pending-transition lanes remain separate. Unit tests check parsing,
failure rejection and recipe boundaries only; fresh native runs and live lockdep
remain qualification gates, including the contended voluntary-shutdown recipe.

Contended voluntary shutdown (private test branch)
-------------------------------------------------

MODE=contended-retain uses two harts, one same-Image cycle and fault-0 firmware.
Only the child receives mk_test_shutdown_contend=1. The private doorbell object
includes native_shutdown_fault.h under MK_NATIVE_TEST_KERNEL; the selector is
default-off and also requires a spawned instance with ID 1. Neither the host nor
the healthy load/unload control enables it.

At normal syscore shutdown a kernel thread acquires the actual overlay mutex,
signals ownership, and holds it while the unmodified qualification trylock is
attempted. The callback releases and waits for the holder after that attempt.
Each completion wait is bounded at five seconds; thread creation, acquisition,
release or timing failure emits MK_TEST_SHUTDOWN_FAIL and cannot qualify a run.
Static completion storage outlives a delayed worker. This is a one-terminal-
shutdown test hook, not a controller interface or a repeatable recovery API.

The raw child action must be poweroff, followed by exactly one
MK_TEST_SHUTDOWN_CONTENDED id=1 held=1 trylock=0 released=1 marker. Only an actual
failed trylock with successful holder release emits it. The existing retention
verifier then requires both assigned harts observed STOPPED, ACTIVE allocation
retention, exec/unload/destroy refusal and host survival. It forbids panic, ACK
loss, automatic reuse, copies/starts/frees and trace loss. The next healthy
shutdown VM must run with the selector absent to retain the positive control.

The hook creates real mutex contention, not a real resource mutation in flight;
it does not qualify every controller race. Host-thread fixtures model kernel
thread/completion boundaries; only a fresh QEMU run qualifies the native path.
Keep this hook, adapter and their build flag out of the production patch series.

Manifest failure modes (private test branch)
-------------------------------------------

MODE=manifest-prepare or manifest-capacity, HARTS=1|2, CYCLES=1 and
FAULT_MODE=0 select a default-off, host-only, instance-1 one-shot kernel fault.
The recipe passes mk_test_manifest_fault=1 or 2; other modes pass 0. Mode 1
returns ENOMEM at the architecture preparation boundary (not a real allocator
exhaustion test). Mode 2 lets preparation succeed, then limits the actual
boot-tree emitter to 128 bytes to exercise ENOSPC. Production branches do not
contain these selectors or their per-object MK_NATIVE_TEST_KERNEL build flag.

Each run must observe all harts STOPPED before Image copies, exact manifest
failure, no HART_START, unchanged ownership and no premature free. Copies are
expected: generic execution copies the stopped Image before finalizing its tree.
The recipe retries the same loaded Image without reloading; actual child boot,
ring roundtrip and copy/start traces are positive controls. Normal halt, all-hart
stop confirmation, unload and deletion follow. verify_manifest.py also requires
correlated Image/control/ring/manifest/pool release markers after stopping.
The Image free must match the loader's physical address and allocated size;
an initrd free cannot substitute for it. Each hart's latest pre-copy status
must be STOPPED in both the failure and retry windows, and retry starts must
follow the last Image/initrd copy.
The shared release verifier now requires image_extent in both retention CONTROL
and manifest RELEASED records and correlates it with the raw loader log. Old
logs lacking that field are not qualified by this stricter verifier; preserve
their original frozen verifier and run the new cases on fresh artifacts.
The focused manifest check runs with and without instrumentation and uses real
libfdt for the small-buffer case. Verifier unit fixtures are not QEMU evidence.
Native qualification requires fresh committed source and matching build artifacts.

Physical-placement rejection (private test branch)
--------------------------------------------------

MODE=placement-negative requires HARTS=1|2, CYCLES=1, no alternate build and
FAULT_MODE=0. Only this mode adds mk_test_bad_placement=1 to the host command
line; the selector is default-off and is not passed to the child. It uses a
valid, unmodified Image and initrd, with no HSM fault selector (mode0) and no
manifest fault. Mode0 firmware may still contain the private test instrumentation.

The private native hook must target host instance 0 loading multikernel
instance 1, once, at the first successful allocation with buf_align==SZ_2M.
Immediately before the existing alignment rejection it changes only the local
physical address to original|PAGE_SIZE (4096), preserving the original virtual
allocation pointer. Required raw printk records, in order, are:

  MK_TEST_PLACEMENT id=1 original=0x... injected=0x... size=DEC align=0x200000
  Multikernel allocation not aligned: phys=0x..., required=0x200000
  MK_TEST_FREE id=1 kind=allocation phys=HEX size=DEC pool=HEX

verify_placement.py requires exactly one injection of an originally 2MiB-aligned
address, a 4096-byte displacement, matching rejection and exactly one free of
the original address and size before retry. The injected address must never
appear as a freed allocation. The valid load must return ENOMEM, leave READY,
both ownership trees and the host boot ID unchanged, and show no copy/start.
The guard log must occur in retained raw dmesg; JSON claims alone cannot pass.

Retry loads the same payload, boots the child and completes a live ring
roundtrip. Separately emitted raw trace windows must match the rejected and
recovered JSON records. The recovery trace includes both load-time copies and
exec-time copies: a trace_marker named MK_PLACEMENT_EXEC separates them. Loading
must copy without starting; exec requires each hart's latest pre-copy status
to be STOPPED and all exec copies to precede the first start. Normal graceful halt,
STOPPED confirmation, unload and deletion follow. PLACEMENT_RELEASED reuses
the manifest lane's healthy Image/control/ring/manifest/pool release proof,
without claiming that a manifest fault ran. Raw loader/resource/free records
must match the executing Image and the two distinct release windows.
The shared lifecycle verifier now requires each hart's latest final observation
to be STOPPED, and correlates raw child boot/release identities with their cycle
records. Preserve historical bundles with their frozen verifiers; these stricter
checks need new evidence, not retroactive relabeling of old runs.

The runner freezes and hashes verify_placement.py with the other verifiers.
It can also be invoked as:

  python3 verify_placement.py SERIAL_LOG EXIT_STATUS HARTS

Local verifier/recipe checks (no kernel build or VM):

  python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tools/testing/selftests/multikernel -p 'test_*.py'
  bash -n tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_lifecycle_test.sh

test_verify_placement.py is a synthetic fixture with false-positive mutations,
not evidence that the native hook, allocator or QEMU lifecycle ran. Native
qualification still requires the private hook, matching fresh build artifacts
and a retained QEMU bundle. This lane does not establish physical cache or
firmware fault behavior.

Ordinary and crash file-kexec regression gates
--------------------------------------------

Run in two separate disposable QEMU VMs:

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_kexec_test.sh \
    KERNEL_BUILD HARNESS KERF_SOURCE RISCV_RUNTIME FIRMWARE OUTPUT_PARENT ordinary
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_kexec_test.sh \
    KERNEL_BUILD HARNESS KERF_SOURCE RISCV_RUNTIME FIRMWARE OUTPUT_PARENT crash

These intentionally replace the primary kernel, unlike the spawn tests that
require the parent to survive. No disk or network is attached. The helper never
executes a host reboot: guest entrypoints refuse execution outside PID1, and
the Python primary also checks RISC-V. Run them only via the QEMU recipe.

Use the same #35/Kerf/runtime pins listed above and OpenSBI without an active
fault selector. OUTPUT_PARENT must end in /build. The runner uses the unchanged
#35 host/secondary initramfs packer in a new directory and keeps failed runs.
Its source must be clean and match KERNEL_BUILD/source-revision.txt; it checks
KERNEL_BUILD/build-artifacts.sha256 relative to the repository root. The exact
selected Image/ELF/config entries must exist, and frozen copies must match those
digests; a manifest covering another build or only config cannot pass. The
original build manifest is retained in the bundle. These are
build-time provenance records: never change a revision stamp to relabel an old
Image. Rebuild the final source and retain its actual hashes before running.

By default the kernel source must be this recipe's checkout. To compare a
separate baseline, append EXPECTED_SOURCE EXPECTED_REVISION (both required):

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_kexec_test.sh \
    KERNEL_BUILD HARNESS KERF_SOURCE RISCV_RUNTIME FIRMWARE OUTPUT_PARENT ordinary \
    EXPECTED_SOURCE EXPECTED_REVISION

EXPECTED_SOURCE must resolve to KERNEL_BUILD/source. EXPECTED_REVISION must be
its full 40-character commit SHA and match both its HEAD and the build stamp.
Both the kernel and recipe checkouts must be clean; a failed status check is
an error, not evidence of cleanliness. The same artifact hash checks apply.
The bundle records source-path.txt/source-revision.txt separately from
recipe-source-path.txt/recipe-revision.txt and freezes qemu_kexec_test.sh too;
all are included in SHA256SUMS. Do not relabel the recipe commit as the kernel
commit, or treat baseline reproduction as final-stack qualification.

Both modes require RV64 KEXEC/KEXEC_FILE, CRASH_RESERVE/CRASH_DUMP, PROC_VMCORE
and MAGIC_SYSRQ built in. They support MK-enabled and MK-disabled builds; neither
creates a multikernel instance or uses the #27/#28 spawn adapters. Both boot with
four harts and 2GiB. Ordinary uses reboot(KEXEC). Crash uses
crashkernel=256M in the primary, checks the actual reservation, loads with
KEXEC_FILE_ON_CRASH, and triggers SysRq-c. Both also set KEXEC_FILE_DEBUG (actual
flags8/10) to record the final entry/type/flags. Purgatory remains enabled when
configured: the recorded entry can differ from the raw Image base and is matched
against machine_kexec's jump, not against the Image base.
The capture kernel gets nr_cpus=1 and a small BusyBox initramfs. The primary's
Python runtime is not in the capture
initramfs. Legacy kexec_load is not covered.

The frozen verifier requires one firmware boot, two kernel boots, correct load
slot/flags, a matching final load/machine_kexec entry address, and ordered
BEGIN/LOADED/HANDOFF/TARGET/PASS records. Each run has an externally checked nonce;
the target must have a different boot ID, the expected release and CPU count.
Crash requires exactly one intended primary SysRq panic, crashdump evidence,
and an actual 64-byte read
of /proc/vmcore with a bounded ELF64 little-endian RISC-V core header. This does
not validate all dump contents or every captured register/page. Timeout, late
failure, unexpected diagnostics, wrong identities and missing stages fail closed.
Post-PASS kernel poweroff evidence is mandatory; reset diagnostics are rejected
because -no-reboot can make a reset terminate QEMU with exit0 too.

The bundle retains the source revision, Image/ELF/config/firmware hashes, both
initramfs files, frozen guest/verifier sources, QEMU version, exact command,
nonce, serial output, verifier output and both process exit statuses. A valid
log without QEMU exit0 is
not a pass. qemu_kexec_guest.py and qemu-kexec-target only run in the guest.

Local checks are verifier fixtures and non-PID1 refusal checks, not runtime proof:

  python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tools/testing/selftests/multikernel -p 'test_*.py'
  bash -n tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu_kexec_test.sh
  sh -n tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/qemu-kexec-target

Both native modes remain unqualified until matching fresh artifacts are booted
and their retained evidence passes verify_kexec.py.

Purgatory relocation regression check
------------------------------------

The independent kernel repair is tested without importing it into this test
branch. Pass its source checkout explicitly (a 64-bit build host is required):

  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/purgatory_reloc_test.sh KERNEL_SOURCE

This compiles the selected kernel's actual relocation handler against a small
ELF fixture. It checks interleaved/reordered HI/LO references, multiple low users,
I/S immediates, addends, section-relative/absolute symbols, displacement limits,
two-byte instruction alignment, rejected matches and surrounding bytes. Run
against the unchanged baseline as a negative control. Optional CC/CFLAGS allow
Clang ASan/UBSan coverage. These are host-side C checks, not a purgatory boot or
ordinary/crash kexec PASS; the separate fresh-build QEMU gates remain required.

Command-line allocation cleanup (private test integration)
--------------------------------------------------------

Run qemu_lifecycle_test.sh with MODE=cmdline-cleanup, HARTS=1 or2, CYCLES=1,
no alternate Image and FAULT=0. This extends the pinned #35 runtime; it adds no
kernel hook or failure-injection interface. Only use a corrected kernel in the
disposable QEMU guest, never the pre-fix kernel or the development host.

The guest first submits one valid Image/initrd with a command-line length that
excludes its terminating NUL. EINVAL alone is insufficient: an isolated tracefs
instance must correlate memdup_user's return to kmalloc and exactly one kfree
for that allocation epoch. A successful load retains the command line visibly
in /proc/kimage until unload; its matching free must follow the unload boundary.
Both operations leave no published Image and preserve assigned resources. The
normal spawn/roundtrip/confirmed-stop sequence then proves subsequent recovery.

The host VM alone receives no_hash_pointers to correlate native allocator events.
This exposes VM kernel addresses in retained logs; do not use it on a real host
or treat the logs as scrubbed. Tracing is PID1-filtered and bounded; per-CPU loss,
missed return probes, missing allocation/free, premature free, unexplained
duplicate frees, wrong scope or missing recovery fail verification. Reallocation
of a freed address starts a different object epoch, not a second command-line
free. Frozen guest helpers/verifiers are included in the bundle checksums.

Local oracle/recipe tests do not qualify native cleanup. Native acceptance needs
a fresh clean-source-bound build, matching firmware, QEMU exit0 and the frozen
verify_cmdline.py result. No allocator tracing is enabled in production ranges.
